      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-hover mb-2">Is it possible to be metabolically healthy if my BMI says I am overweight?</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">
            Yes, absolutely and scientifically documented. This is often termed"metabolically healthy obesity," although in the case of active individuals, it is due to high lean muscle mass. If your cardiovascular markers (LDL/HDL cholesterol, triglycerides, fasting glucose, insulin sensitivity, and blood pressure) are completely optimal, and your actual body fat percentage (measured) is firmly anchored within athletic or healthy ranges, a high BMI derived exclusively from muscular hyperdevelopment poses zero clinical risk to your life expectancy.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-hover mb-2">How often should I really step on the scale to check my recomposition progress?</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">
            Constant daily weighing can generate enormous psychological fatigue in a high percentage of individuals due to logical erratic fluctuations (water retention and digestive cycle). Expert nutritionists strongly recommend weighing yourself only under identical and strict biological and environmental conditions: completely fasted, right after the first morning bowel movement/urination, always wearing no clothes, and performing this check exactly 1 or 2 strict times a week, noting the weighted average over the prolonged monthly period to discern the true trend of the physical change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-hover mb-2">How does the so-called"bone constitution," or the size of my skeleton, affect the medical goals of healthy weight ranges?</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">
            Although standardized BMI estimates are rudimentarily designed to offer a good approximate fit for the vast general populations of individuals, they ignore biological exceptions. People genetically endowed with unusually wide shoulder girdles, long femurs, and highly thick joints will logically present significantly higher structural weights than others of their exact height. It is for this and no other reason that truly sophisticated clinical models strongly advise focusing patients' diagnostic resources on their exact proportions of fat and muscle tissue hypertrophy as infinitely superior metrics of objective long-term health, relegating statistical BMI to a second and less vital step of importance in serious consultations.
          </p>
        </div>
